WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.522
You need a name and a one-liner people can repeat.

00:00:03.522 --> 00:00:05.283
Not a branding theater project.

00:00:05.283 --> 00:00:07.748
This lesson is about name and one-liner.

00:00:07.748 --> 00:00:09.157
Memorable without branding theater.

00:00:09.157 --> 00:00:11.623
Do not treat this as theory only.

00:00:11.623 --> 00:00:15.145
Treat it as a move you can run this week.

00:00:15.145 --> 00:00:20.428
By the end, you should be able to act without waiting for a perfect plan.

00:00:20.428 --> 00:00:24.302
Keep the DigProg habit: watch, practice, then take one real action.

00:00:24.302 --> 00:00:28.176
Stay practical: write a one-liner before you optimize branding or tools.

00:00:28.496 --> 00:00:32.870
Pick a simple name you can say on a call without apologizing.

00:00:32.870 --> 00:00:35.057
Remember the objective: write a one-liner.

00:00:35.057 --> 00:00:39.066
Write the idea in your own words before you move on.

00:00:39.066 --> 00:00:43.440
Name one concrete next action you can finish in under an hour.

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:47.449
Avoid vanity work that feels busy but does not create proof.

00:00:47.449 --> 00:00:51.823
If something is unclear, simplify the promise and try a smaller version.

00:00:51.823 --> 00:00:54.374
Talk to a real person when possible.

00:00:54.374 --> 00:00:55.832
Feedback beats guessing alone.

00:00:56.152 --> 00:01:01.279
Write a one-liner: who it helps, what outcome, and how it is different enough.

